NEW2=[Paragraph("6. Robustness checks",H),Paragraph(f"Because Model A assumes a linear trend and normal errors, three additional checks were run. A rank correlation and a bootstrap of the year slope both point the same direction as the OLS result: later releases peak lower on Hot Latin Songs. The era comparison also shows that peaks differ across eras. However, dropping the 2022 releases (a burst of many Legendaddy-era tracks, most peaking low) weakens the rank correlation to rho={R2['rho_no22']} (p={R2['p_no22']}), so part of the apparent decline comes from release volume and low-peaking album-cycle tracks in a single year rather than from a steady fall in his best songs. A fair reading is: a downward drift in typical peak exists, it is sensitive to that cluster, and it does not mean his top songs stopped performing (Section 3 shows number-one entries in 2019).",B),tab(_r,[3.4*inch,2.8*inch]),Spacer(1,6),
Paragraph("7. Album sales and certifications",H),Paragraph(f"US album sales in the source table fall from 1,083,000 for Barrio Fino (2004) and 809,000 for its live companion to 50,000 for Prestige (2012), and King Daddy (2013) is a digital-only mixtape at 2,000. This tracks the industry shift from physical sales to streaming and singles, which is also why single-level certifications dominate later: the highest RIAA multipliers in this dataset belong to singles from 2004 and 2016-2020. Of the {R2['n_h100']} songs here with a recorded Hot 100 position, they are spread across eras (Global 5, Collab 1, Streaming 8, Farewell 3), with the best at number 1. RIAA Latin and standard certifications use different unit thresholds, so multipliers should not be compared across the two categories; the source table does not always say which applies, and Legendaddy and the early albums have no sales figure listed.",B)]+fig("f7_sales.png","Figure 7. US album sales listed in the source table.")+fig("f8_certs.png","Figure 8. Highest RIAA multipliers among songs in the dataset.")+[tab(_c,[3.4*inch,2.8*inch]),Spacer(1,6)]
NEW=[Paragraph("8. Collaboration network",H),Paragraph(f"Credited collaborators on {NET['n_songs']} collaborative songs form a set of {NET['n_collab']} distinct acts. The most frequent partners in this dataset are: Anuel AA (6), Ozuna (6), Wisin & Yandel (6), Natti Natasha (6), Sech (5), J Balvin (4). Wisin &amp; Yandel is counted as one duo node, separate from Wisin and Yandel credited individually. Using co-appearance on the same song as an edge, the most central nodes by degree centrality are: "+", ".join(NET["central"])+". Frequent partners are concentrated in 2016-2022, the same period when the share of collaborative releases rises, which fits the era table above. This is a descriptive map of credits, not a causal estimate of what any partner contributed.",B),tab(_t,[2*inch,.8*inch,1*inch,1.2*inch]),Spacer(1,6)]+fig("f6_network.png","Figure 6. Top collaborators by song count and the co-appearance network of collaborators with 3 or more links.")+[Paragraph("9. Streaming and attention milestones",H),Paragraph("A consistent per-song streaming time series was not available from the sources used. The table below collects press-reported milestones only; the figures come from different dates and outlets, are not comparable with each other, and should be re-checked before citation. Weeks-on-chart data was likewise not collected, so a chart-longevity (survival) model is left for future work.",B),tab(_s,[1.6*inch,2*inch,1.2*inch,1.2*inch]),Spacer(1,6)]
s=[Paragraph("Daddy Yankee: A Quantitative Career Analysis",S["Title"]),Paragraph("Chart trajectory, collaboration roles and career eras, 2002-2025",C),Spacer(1,8),
Paragraph("Abstract",H),Paragraph(f"This paper builds a song-level dataset of {R['n_songs']} Daddy Yankee releases (lead singles, collaborations and featured appearances) from a public discography table and tests three descriptive questions: how his Billboard Hot Latin Songs performance changed across career eras, whether collaboration role is associated with chart peak, and whether year and role can predict a Latin top-10 entry. Of {R['n_latin']} songs with a recorded Hot Latin peak, {R['n_top10']} reached the top 10 and {R['n_no1']} reached number one. Peak position drifts downward (worse) over time in a linear model, but the role effects are statistically indistinguishable from zero and the logistic model does not beat the base rate. The results are descriptive; the sample is small and drawn from one secondary source.",B),
Paragraph("1. Data and method",H),Paragraph("Sources: the Wikipedia 'Daddy Yankee discography' page (which cites Billboard, RIAA and other chart bodies), retrieved November 2026. Each row records title, year, role (solo lead, lead with collaborators, featured artist), album, Billboard Hot 100 peak, Hot Latin Songs peak and a selected certification. Songs without a listed Hot Latin position are treated as not charting there, which is a limitation (they may have charted on other Billboard lists). Album tracks that were never released as singles are not included, so this is a singles-and-features dataset, not an exhaustive track list. Models: (a) ordinary least squares on Hot Latin peak among charted songs; (b) logistic regression for a Latin top-10 entry across all songs, with a small ridge penalty. Predictors are release year (centered at 2004) and role indicators with solo lead as the reference.",B)]
s+=fig("f5_eras.png","Figure 1. Career eras used in this study.")
s+=[Paragraph("2. Career structure",H),Paragraph("Early 1990s mixtape work with DJ Playero led to the 1995 debut No Mercy and underground compilations. El Cangri.com (2002) broadened his audience, and Barrio Fino (2004) became the first reggaeton album to debut and peak at number one on Top Latin Albums, with Gasolina's success cited as the reason for a new Latin Rhythm Airplay format. The 2017 Despacito collaboration reached number one on the Hot 100 for 16 weeks. He announced retirement in March 2022, released Legendaddy that year, and issued Christian-market releases in 2025 (Lamento en Baile peaked at number 15 on Top Christian Albums).",B)]
g=pd.DataFrame(R["era"]);d=[["Era","Charted songs","Median Latin peak","No. 1","Top 10"]]+[[r.era,int(r.n),r.median_peak,int(r.no1),int(r.top10)] for r in g.itertuples()]
s+=[Paragraph("3. Chart performance by era",H),tab(d,[1.5*inch,1.1*inch,1.4*inch,.7*inch,.7*inch]),Spacer(1,6)]+fig("f2_era.png","Figure 2. Top-10 and number-one Hot Latin entries per era.")+fig("f1_scatter.png","Figure 3. Hot Latin peak by release year and role, with the solo-lead OLS trend.")
s+=[Paragraph(f"Median Latin peak worsens from {g.median_peak.iloc[0]} in the Global era to {g.median_peak.iloc[-2]} in the Farewell era. This partly reflects a larger volume of releases per year in later eras, where more songs mean more low-peaking entries, so the median drops even if his best songs do not.",B),PageBreak(),Paragraph("4. Models",H)]
rows=[["Term","Coefficient","Std. error","t"]]+[[o["names"][i],o["b"][i],o["se"][i],round(zo[i],2)] for i in range(4)]
s+=[Paragraph(f"<b>Model A: OLS, Hot Latin peak (n={o['n']}, R-squared={o['r2']}).</b> Higher peak numbers are worse.",B),tab(rows,[2*inch,1.2*inch,1.2*inch,.8*inch]),Spacer(1,4),Paragraph(f"Each later year is associated with a peak about {o['b'][1]} positions worse, a modest trend (|t|={abs(zo[1]):.1f}). The collaboration and featured coefficients are small relative to their standard errors, so this data gives no evidence that role changes peak position once year is held fixed. R-squared is low: year and role explain little of the variation.",B)]
rows=[["Term","Log-odds","Std. error","Odds ratio"]]+[[l["names"][i],l["b"][i],l["se"][i],l["or"][i]] for i in range(4)]
s+=[Paragraph(f"<b>Model B: logistic, Latin top-10 entry (n={l['n']}).</b>",B),tab(rows,[2*inch,1.2*inch,1.2*inch,1*inch]),Spacer(1,4)]+fig("f3_role.png","Figure 4. Share of charted songs reaching the Latin top 10, by role.")+fig("f4_logit.png","Figure 5. Logistic coefficients with 95% intervals; all intervals include zero.")
s+=[Paragraph(f"All three intervals cross zero. In-sample accuracy is {l['acc']}, equal to the base rate of {l['base']} from always predicting no top-10 entry, so the model adds no predictive value. Featured appearances have the highest raw top-10 share in this set, but with 22 songs the difference is within noise.",B),
*NEW2,*NEW,Paragraph("10. Limitations and next steps",H),Paragraph("(1) One secondary source, hand-transcribed; verify against Billboard before citing. (2) Survivorship and selection: only singles and features are included, and non-charting is inferred from blank cells. (3) Small n and an un-modeled rise in release volume. (4) No streaming or tour time series; album sales cover seven US releases only, and Latin and standard RIAA thresholds differ. Natural extensions: a consistent per-song streaming time series and weeks-on-chart data, which would allow a survival model of chart longevity.",B),
Paragraph("References",H),Paragraph("Wikipedia, 'Daddy Yankee discography' (citing Billboard, RIAA, AMPROFON, PROMUSICAE and others); Billboard artist chart history pages for Daddy Yankee. Press milestones: GRAMMY.com, Metro PR, RPP/EFE, Fox 10, CBC. Data and code: see the repository (data/ and code/).",B)]
